      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "InSapphoWeTrust from Los Angeles, California, USA",
      "description": "Calico, a ghost town located in Yermo halfway between Los Angeles and Las Vegas, was once a very productive silver mining town, home to over 3,000 people in the 1880s. The decline in silver prices killed the mines in 1896, and the town shriveled to nothing. Today's Calico is a 1950s reconstruction by Walter Knott (of Knott's Berry Farm in Buena Park) on top of the original building foundations, donated to the County of San Bernardino in 1967.\n\nHere is a mine entrance. Calico was a productive mining town for only 15 years - 1881 to 1896. And the reconstructed mine entrance's sign tells it all; Rhyolite is located in Nevada, and well to the north of Calico. Today, due to the danger of collapse, all abandoned mines are off-limits to the public, except for Maggie Mine, which is one of the key attractions of today's reconstructed Calico.",
